WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.580
Where should an AI
agent be allowed to run?

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.730
Sooner or later, one will follow
an instruction we did not give.

00:00:07.555 --> 00:00:09.485
A sandbox can fall short in four ways.

00:00:10.817 --> 00:00:12.807
First, the agent runs as you.

00:00:13.057 --> 00:00:18.187
On a work laptop, it has that person's
files, saved passwords and cloud keys.

00:00:18.582 --> 00:00:21.972
One hidden line in a web
page or ticket can steer it.

00:00:22.152 --> 00:00:26.202
NCSC UK advises safeguards
outside the model.

00:00:26.442 --> 00:00:30.972
And one vendor's documentation
says its sandbox is off by default.

00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:34.413
Second, a container is a thin wall.

00:00:34.708 --> 00:00:37.858
Every container on a machine
talks to the same kernel.

00:00:38.188 --> 00:00:43.218
A microVM or a virtual machine
gives the agent a kernel of its own.

00:00:43.423 --> 00:00:48.193
For code an AI wrote after reading the
internet, consider a stronger wall.

00:00:49.217 --> 00:00:52.967
Third, the door to the internet is open,
and the keys are inside.

00:00:53.432 --> 00:00:56.782
A hijacked agent can send
what it reads to any site.

00:00:57.082 --> 00:00:58.952
Two settings close most of this.

00:00:59.227 --> 00:01:03.397
Block outbound traffic by default,
with a short list of allowed sites.

00:01:03.762 --> 00:01:08.632
And keep the real keys outside, handing
in a short-lived token for each task.

00:01:09.783 --> 00:01:12.033
Finally, the pilot that never ended.

00:01:12.318 --> 00:01:14.728
It starts with test data and an end date.

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:17.723
Then someone copies in real customer data.

00:01:17.998 --> 00:01:21.828
Months later, it is in daily use,
with no owner and no logs.

00:01:22.223 --> 00:01:25.293
Personal data in a pilot
is still personal data.

00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:27.527
What good looks like.

00:01:27.812 --> 00:01:31.842
A throwaway machine for each task,
with nothing of value inside.

00:01:32.277 --> 00:01:34.187
A wall matched to what runs in it.

00:01:34.387 --> 00:01:37.497
Outbound blocked by default,
and no standing keys.

00:01:37.932 --> 00:01:41.942
A named person approves merging,
deploying, sending and paying.

00:01:42.262 --> 00:01:47.472
OWASP lists unexpected code execution
among the top ten risks for agents.

00:01:49.397 --> 00:01:50.607
A suggested first step.

00:01:50.892 --> 00:01:55.042
Check whether agents run sandboxed today,
rather than assuming it.

00:01:55.242 --> 00:01:58.432
Then give them a machine of their own,
not someone's laptop.

00:01:58.912 --> 00:02:01.042
And set three rules for every pilot:

00:02:01.692 --> 00:02:04.442
fake data, an end date,
one route to production.

00:02:04.847 --> 00:02:06.377
Where to start is your call.

00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:11.207
Give the agent nothing of value,
and nowhere to send it.

